    {
      "page_title": "Volumes and Storage",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Why Storage Matters",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "The container's ==writable layer== is ephemeral: `docker rm` deletes it, and copy-on-write makes it slower than a volume.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Writes copy the file up from the image layer first",
            "Data dies with the container, so never keep state there",
            "Volumes bypass the union filesystem for native speed"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Three Mount Types",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "==Named volume==: managed by Docker in `/var/lib/docker/volumes`; preferred for persistence",
            "==Bind mount==: maps a host path in; great for dev hot reload",
            "==tmpfs==: memory only, gone on stop; good for secrets and scratch",
            "Bind mounts depend on host layout and permissions"
          ],
          "style": "star",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              {
                "text": "Container",
                "sub": "writes to /data",
                "ink": "blue"
              },
              {
                "text": "Mount",
                "sub": "volume | bind | tmpfs",
                "ink": "purple"
              },
              {
                "text": "Storage",
                "sub": "Docker dir | host | RAM",
                "ink": "green"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Mount Comparison",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Type",
            "Lives in",
            "Survives rm",
            "Best for"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Named volume",
              "Docker area",
              "Yes",
              "Databases"
            ],
            [
              "Bind mount",
              "Host path",
              "Yes",
              "Dev hot reload"
            ],
            [
              "tmpfs",
              "RAM",
              "No",
              "Secrets, scratch"
            ],
            [
              "Anonymous volume",
              "Docker area",
              "Orphaned",
              "Avoid"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 0,
          "frame": "dashed"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. -v vs --mount",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "docker run -v ./app:/app img",
            "# -v creates a missing dir",
            "docker run --mount \\",
            "  type=bind,src=./app,dst=/app img",
            "# --mount errors if src missing"
          ],
          "note": "-v silently creates missing sources"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`--mount` is explicit: key=value pairs, fails fast",
            "`:ro` makes the mount read-only",
            "A new volume is ==initialized== with the image's content at that path on first use"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Managing Volumes",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "docker volume create pgdata",
            "docker volume ls",
            "docker volume inspect pgdata",
            "docker volume rm pgdata",
            "docker run --volumes-from db app"
          ],
          "note": "--volumes-from shares another's mounts"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Anonymous volumes get random names and pile up",
            "`docker volume prune` removes unused ones",
            "Volume drivers (NFS, cloud block storage) give shared or remote storage"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Interview Point",
          "items": [
            "Volume vs bind mount vs tmpfs: when each?",
            "Why does a bind mount hide node_modules?",
            "How would you back up a named volume?",
            "Why is --mount safer than -v?"
          ],
          "ink": "blue"
        }
      ],
      "hero": "docker",
      "subtitle": "Persisting container data"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Backups, Drivers and Cleanup",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Backup a Volume",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "docker run --rm \\",
            "  -v pgdata:/data:ro \\",
            "  -v $PWD:/backup alpine \\",
            "  tar czf /backup/pg.tgz /data"
          ],
          "note": "throwaway container, tar to bind mount"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Common Mistakes",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Bind mount over `/app/node_modules` **hides** the image's installed copy",
            "Using the writable layer for database files",
            "Relying on `-v` and mistyping a source path",
            "Never pruning anonymous volumes"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Restore a Volume",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "docker run --rm \\",
            "  -v pgdata:/data \\",
            "  -v $PWD:/backup alpine \\",
            "  tar xzf /backup/pg.tgz -C /"
          ],
          "note": "mount rw this time, extract at root"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Stop the database container first for a consistent copy",
            "Restore into a ==fresh named volume== to test the backup",
            "A backup never restored is only a **hope**"
          ],
          "style": "check",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Volume Drivers",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Driver",
            "Backed by",
            "Use"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "local",
              "Docker host disk",
              "Default"
            ],
            [
              "local + NFS",
              "NFS server",
              "Shared mounts"
            ],
            [
              "Cloud plugin",
              "Block storage",
              "Remote volumes"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 0,
          "frame": "dashed"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Pick a driver with `docker volume create -d`",
            "Remote drivers let a container move hosts and keep data",
            "Latency of remote storage hits database workloads hardest"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Permission Denied",
          "ink": "orange"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "The container user's ==UID== must match the file owner",
            "Bind mounts keep **host** ownership; root-owned files block non-root apps",
            "Fix with `chown` on the host or the `--user` flag"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "orange"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "docker run --rm -u 1000:1000 \\",
            "  -v appdata:/data alpine \\",
            "  touch /data/ok"
          ],
          "note": "fails if UID 1000 can't write"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Inspect and Clean Up",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "docker system df -v",
            "docker volume ls -f dangling=true",
            "docker volume prune"
          ],
          "note": "prune deletes data permanently"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Prune Safely",
          "text": "Recent Docker prunes only anonymous volumes; `-a` also removes unused named ones. List first, then prune."
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Interview Point",
          "items": [
            "How do you restore a volume from a tarball?",
            "Why do bind mounts cause permission errors?",
            "What does docker volume prune delete?"
          ],
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Key Takeaways",
          "text": "Persist with named volumes, bind-mount for dev, tmpfs for secrets, and prefer --mount."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "docker",
      "subtitle": "Persisting container data"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Docker Networking",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Built-in Drivers",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "==bridge== is the default: NAT networking on one host",
            "==host== shares the host stack: no isolation, no port mapping, fastest on Linux",
            "==none== gives only loopback, no outside access",
            "==overlay== spans multiple hosts, used by Swarm",
            "==macvlan== gives each container its own MAC on the LAN"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Publishing a Port",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              {
                "text": "Client",
                "sub": "host:8080"
              },
              {
                "text": "iptables",
                "sub": "DNAT rule",
                "ink": "red"
              },
              {
                "text": "docker0",
                "sub": "bridge"
              },
              {
                "text": "Container",
                "sub": ":80",
                "ink": "green"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`-p host:container` installs the ==iptables DNAT== rule",
            "`EXPOSE` is only documentation, it opens nothing",
            "`-p 127.0.0.1:8080:80` keeps the port off other interfaces"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Default vs User Bridge",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Feature",
            "Default docker0",
            "User-defined"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Name DNS",
              "None",
              "Yes, 127.0.0.11"
            ],
            [
              "Outbound",
              "NAT out",
              "NAT out"
            ],
            [
              "Isolation",
              "All share one",
              "Per network"
            ],
            [
              "Created by",
              "Built in",
              "`network create`"
            ]
          ],
          "frame": "dashed"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Peers and Host",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Container to container: use the ==service name== on a shared network",
            "Never use the container IP, it changes on restart",
            "Desktop reaches the host via `host.docker.internal`",
            "Linux needs `--add-host=host.docker.internal:host-gateway`"
          ],
          "style": "check",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Network Commands",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "docker run -d --name web nginx",
            "docker network create appnet",
            "docker network connect appnet web",
            "docker network inspect appnet"
          ],
          "note": "disconnect undoes connect; many networks allowed"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Common Mistakes",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "App listens on `127.0.0.1` inside: published ports never connect, bind **0.0.0.0**",
            "Published ports bypass ufw and firewalld: Docker edits iptables directly",
            "Hardcoding container IPs instead of names"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Interview Point",
          "items": [
            "Why can't default bridge containers resolve each other by name?",
            "What does -p 8080:80 actually do on the host?",
            "Does EXPOSE publish a port? Why or why not?",
            "How do you reach the host from a Linux container?"
          ],
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Key Takeaways",
          "text": "Use user-defined networks and names, publish only what you need, and bind apps to 0.0.0.0."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "docker",
      "subtitle": "Drivers, DNS and ports"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Docker Compose",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "☆ 1. What Compose Is",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "One ==compose.yaml== defines a multi-container app; `docker compose up` runs it",
            "Top-level keys: `services`, `networks`, `volumes`, `configs`, `secrets`",
            "Service fields: `image`, `build`, `ports`, `environment`, `env_file`",
            "More fields: `volumes`, `depends_on`, `command`, `restart`",
            "Compose v2 is the `docker compose` plugin; v1 `docker-compose` is retired"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "☆ 2. Network and Readiness",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Compose creates a default project network; services reach each other by ==service name==",
            "`depends_on` controls start order only, not readiness",
            "Add `condition: service_healthy` plus a healthcheck to wait",
            "`healthcheck`: `test`, `interval`, `timeout`, `retries`, `start_period`"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              {
                "text": "db starts",
                "sub": "container running",
                "ink": "blue"
              },
              {
                "text": "healthcheck",
                "sub": "test every interval",
                "ink": "orange"
              },
              {
                "text": "healthy",
                "sub": "within retries",
                "ink": "green"
              },
              {
                "text": "web starts",
                "sub": "service_healthy met",
                "ink": "purple"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "depends_on form",
            "Waits for",
            "Use"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`db` (short)",
              "Container started",
              "Order only"
            ],
            [
              "`service_started`",
              "Container started",
              "Same as short"
            ],
            [
              "`service_healthy`",
              "Healthcheck passes",
              "Databases"
            ],
            [
              "`service_completed_successfully`",
              "Job exits 0",
              "Seed jobs"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 2,
          "frame": "dashed"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "☆ 3. Config Layers",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`${VAR:-default}` reads the shell or `.env`, else the default",
            "`.env` feeds substitution in the YAML; `env_file` loads vars into the container",
            "Profiles start optional services: `profiles: [debug]`, then `--profile debug`",
            "`compose.override.yaml` merges automatically on `up`",
            "Layer prod over base: `-f compose.yaml -f compose.prod.yaml`"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "☆ 4. Example: Wait for DB",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "yaml",
          "lines": [
            "# db defines a healthcheck",
            "web:",
            "  depends_on:",
            "    db:",
            "      condition: service_healthy"
          ],
          "note": "ready, not just started"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "☆ 6. Common Mistakes",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`down -v` deletes named volumes and your ==database data==",
            "Plain `depends_on` assumes the DB is ready; it only waits for **start**",
            "Running `docker-compose` v1 instead of the `docker compose` plugin",
            "Expecting `.env` to reach containers; that job belongs to `env_file`"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Interview Point",
          "items": [
            "depends_on vs healthcheck: how does web wait for its DB?",
            "How do services in one Compose file find each other?",
            "What does `down -v` remove that `down` keeps?"
          ],
          "ink": "blue"
        }
      ],
      "hero": "docker",
      "subtitle": "Multi-container apps"
    }
